---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { cycleRange, generatedAt, members, partyLetter } from "../lib/data";
import { congress, lobbyingSpan, ordinal, caucusOf, partyPlural } from "../lib/legislation";
import { filingSpan, revolvingStats, REVIEW } from "../lib/revolving";
import { faraSince } from "../lib/fara";

// "Bernard Sanders and Angus S. King, Jr. with Democrats, and Kevin Kiley with Republicans"
const independents = members.filter((m) => partyLetter(m.party) === "I" && caucusOf(m));
const byCaucus = ["D", "R"]
  .map((c) => ({ c, names: independents.filter((m) => caucusOf(m) === c).map((m) => m.name) }))
  .filter((x) => x.names.length)
  .map((x) => `${x.names.join(" and ")} with ${partyPlural(x.c)}`)
  .join(", and ");
---

<Base
  title="About the data: sources, methods and what's counted"
  og={{ type: "page", id: "about" }}
  description={`Where contributions.fyi's campaign finance data comes from (FEC, SEC EDGAR and lobbying disclosures), what's counted, ${cycleRange}, and how PACs are matched to organizations and industries.`}
  crumbs={[["About the data", "/about/"]]}
>
  <p class="kicker">About the data</p>
  <h1>How this data works</h1>
  <p class="dek">
    Every number here comes from public filings: the FEC for money, SEC EDGAR and lobbying disclosures for who's
    behind each PAC. Here's what's counted, and what isn't.
  </p>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What's counted</h2>
    <p>
      Direct contributions from political committees (PACs, leadership PACs, party and candidate committees)
      to the campaigns of current members of Congress, {cycleRange}, as reported to the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.fec.gov/data/browse-data/?tab=bulk-data">Federal Election Commission</a>.
      Each member's page shows how much they raised from individuals in total, but individual donors are only
      named when they give large sums to outside spenders (below).
    </p>
    <p>
      A PAC's money comes from the employees, members or owners of the organization that sponsors it,
      not from the organization's treasury. Corporations can't give directly to federal candidates.
    </p>

    <h2>Outside spending and big donors</h2>
    <p>
      Super PACs and other groups can raise and spend unlimited amounts for or against candidates, as long as they
      don't coordinate with the campaign. These <em>independent expenditures</em> are shown separately from
      contributions, split into spending supporting and opposing each member, because money spent attacking
      someone isn't money given to them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside spending comes from three kinds of FEC filings. PACs' and super PACs' periodic reports are the record, as the
      FEC processes them. Before an election, committees must also file <em>24- and 48-hour notices</em> within days of
      each ad buy; a notice is counted only when it's dated after the committee's last processed report, so the same
      expenditure is never counted from both. Groups and people that aren't political committees (nonprofits such as
      501(c)(4)s, unions, trade groups, individuals) report independent expenditures on{" "}
      <a href="https://www.fec.gov/help-candidates-and-committees/making-independent-expenditures/reporting-independent-expenditures-form-5/">Form 5</a>,
      counted the same way: their quarterly reports, plus notices dated after the latest one. Amended filings replace
      the versions they amend.
    </p>
    <p>
      Form 5 asks only for donations given to pay for independent expenditures, so many groups that file it report none.
      Where a group's reports list no donors at all, its page says that it doesn't disclose its donors.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Electioneering communications</em> (Form 9) are broadcast ads that name a candidate shortly before an
      election without saying vote for or against them. They're listed apart from spending for and against, with each
      candidate's share of an ad that named several, as the FEC calculates it.
    </p>
    <p>
      For every group that spent on current members (other than party committees and campaigns), donations of
      $50,000 or more are listed as reported to the FEC. A donor's money isn't traced to particular ads: once given, it's pooled with
      everyone else's.
    </p>

    <h2>Small donors, home state and self-funding</h2>
    <p>
      Campaigns itemize every donor who gives more than $200 in a year and report the rest as a single total. The
      split between those comes from the FEC's committee summaries for each member's own campaign committees. Where
      itemized donors live comes from the address on each donation: in the member's state or not, and for House members,
      in their district or not. ZIP codes are placed in today's districts with the Census Bureau's{" "}
      <a href="https://www2.census.gov/geo/docs/maps-data/data/rel2020/cd-sld/">relationship file</a>; a ZIP that crosses
      district lines is split by land area, so the district figure is an estimate. Self-funding is the candidate's own
      contributions and loans to the campaign, from the FEC candidate summaries; loans may be repaid. Each figure is
      compared with the median member of the same chamber.
    </p>

    <h2>Lobbyist bundling</h2>
    <p>
      When a registered lobbyist or a lobbyist's PAC collects contributions from others and passes them to a campaign
      or leadership PAC, the committee must report it on{" "}
      <a href="https://www.fec.gov/help-candidates-and-committees/lobbyist-bundling-disclosure/">Form 3L</a> once the
      bundles pass a threshold, indexed for inflation, in a half-year. These come from the electronic filings. The money
      itself is already counted with the donors who gave it. A bundler is linked to an organization here only when its
      PAC is one of ours or the lobbyist's employer matches an organization's name exactly.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is behind each PAC</h2>
    <p>Each PAC is traced to the organization behind it, in this order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>FEC filings</strong> name a PAC's connected organization and its type (corporation, labor union, trade association, membership group), and the members of Congress who run leadership PACs.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.sec.gov/edgar">SEC EDGAR</a></strong> gives an industry code for publicly traded companies.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://lda.gov/">Lobbying disclosures</a></strong> list the issues an organization lobbies on, and which PACs belong to which lobbying registrants.</li>
      <li>Keywords in the organization's name (“Association of Realtors”, “Physicians”) for associations with no SEC or lobbying record.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      These feed a fixed set of sectors. PACs with no sponsoring organization are counted as{" "}
      <em>Ideological &amp; Single-Issue</em>. Some classifications are set by hand; the rules are
      in the project's source code. Mistakes are possible, so check the linked FEC filings for anything important.
    </p>

    <h2 id="bills">Bills, votes and lobbying</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/bills/">Bills</a> covers every bill and joint or concurrent resolution the House or Senate held a recorded
      vote on passing in the {ordinal(congress)} Congress. Bill details come from the Government Publishing Office's{" "}
      <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS">bill status data</a> (the same records behind congress.gov),
      and votes from the <a href="https://clerk.house.gov/Votes">House Clerk</a> and the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.senate.gov/legislative/votes_new.htm">Senate</a>. Only votes on passing a measure are shown,
      including agreeing to the other chamber's version and veto overrides. Procedural votes and amendments are left out:
      they mostly follow party lines whatever the bill says.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each vote is labeled <strong>party-line</strong> when 90% or more of each party voted on opposite sides,{" "}
      <strong>near-unanimous</strong> when 90% or more of both voted the same way, and <strong>split</strong> otherwise.
      Members are counted under the party they belonged to when they voted, as recorded on the roll call.{" "}
      <strong>Independents</strong> are shown as independents, but counted with the party they caucus with on the day of
      the vote, from the{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators">congress-legislators</a> project
      {byCaucus && <>: today, {byCaucus}</>}. A member who switched to independent mid-Congress is counted under their
      old party for the votes before the switch. An independent who caucuses with neither party would be counted on
      their own. On member pages, "broke with party" means siding with a quarter of their party (or caucus) or less.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lobbying comes from the quarterly LD-2 reports lobbyists file on <a href="https://lda.gov/">lda.gov</a>,{" "}
      {lobbyingSpan}. Each report describes the issues lobbied on in free text, and about a quarter of them name bills by
      number ("H.R. 3633", "S.1582"). An organization is listed on a bill's page when one of its reports names the bill.
      A citation is dropped when the bill hadn't been introduced by the end of the report's quarter (bill numbers start
      over every Congress, so that's an older bill with the same number), or when the filer wrote a name beside the
      number that's plainly a different bill. Some typos will still get through.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things the reports don't say. They don't say <strong>which side</strong> an organization took: an organization
      listed on a bill may have backed it, opposed it, or wanted part of it changed, and fewer than one in ten reports say
      which. And they don't say <strong>how much was spent on any one bill</strong>: a report's spending covers every
      issue lobbied on that quarter. So this site doesn't put an organization's PAC money next to how members voted on
      the bills it lobbied on. Reports name what was lobbied on, not what was paid for.
    </p>

    <h2 id="revolving-door">Former staff who lobby</h2>
    <p>
      Lobbyists must list any "covered position" they held in the 20 years before they first lobbied for a client: jobs
      in a member's office, on a committee's staff, in congressional leadership, or senior executive-branch jobs. They
      appear on LD-1 registrations and LD-2 quarterly reports on <a href="https://lda.gov/">lda.gov</a>; the{" "}
      <a href="/revolving-door/">revolving door</a> pages read every one filed {filingSpan}. Of{" "}
      {revolvingStats.lobbyists.toLocaleString()} lobbyists on those filings,{" "}
      {revolvingStats.withPositions.toLocaleString()} listed at least one job.
    </p>
    <p>
      The jobs are written by hand, in any form: "Chief of Staff, Rep. Jane Doe", "LD to Sen. Doe (R-TX)", "Counsel,
      Senate Finance Cmte." Each is split into separate jobs and searched for a member's name after a title ("Rep.",
      "Senator", "Leader") or a first name. A job is credited to a member only when exactly one person fits among
      everyone who has served in Congress since 2003, so a staffer for a retired senator who shares a current senator's
      last name isn't credited to the current one, and "Sen. Scott" alone, with two Senator Scotts, isn't credited to
      either. A senator's former House staff count, and say so. Committees are matched by name or a common abbreviation
      ("Ways and Means", "HASC"), and when both chambers have one by that name, only when the job says which.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a hand check of {REVIEW.members.checked} randomly chosen member matches, {REVIEW.members.right} were the right
      member; of {REVIEW.committees.checked} committee matches, {REVIEW.committees.right} were the right committee. Matching
      favors being right over being complete, so some former staff are missed, especially of members who share a last
      name with another member and whose staff didn't write a first name. A job on the list may be decades old, and
      working in an office doesn't mean working for the member directly.
    </p>

    <h2 id="foreign-lobbying">Foreign lobbying</h2>
    <p>
      People and firms in the US who lobby, do public relations or raise money for a foreign government, political
      party or company register with the Justice Department under the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.justice.gov/nsd-fara">Foreign Agents Registration Act</a>. <a href="/foreign-lobbying/">Foreign
      lobbying</a> uses the department's <a href="https://efile.fara.gov/ords/fara/f?p=API:BULKDATA">bulk data</a>:
      registrants, the foreign principals each represents (with the country it lists), and the short forms filed by
      each person working on a registration. Registrations in effect any time since {faraSince.slice(0, 4)} are included.
    </p>
    <p>
      Registrants report political contributions twice a year on supplemental statements, but only as PDF documents,
      many of them scanned, so the donations here come from the FEC's itemized records instead: donations of more than
      $200 to current members' campaigns, from a person with a FARA short form in effect on the date of the donation,
      who gave the registrant as their employer, with a matching first and last name. Supplemental statements sometimes
      list donations from others at the firm who aren't registered; those aren't counted. FARA doesn't tie a donation to
      any one client, so donations are shown by person and firm, never by country.
    </p>
    <p>
      Registrants are linked to organizations on this site, and to lobbying registrations under the Lobbying Disclosure
      Act, only when the names are the same after dropping suffixes like "LLP" or "Inc.", or when the organization is
      the firm's PAC ("Akin Gump Strauss Hauer &amp; Feld LLP Civic Action Committee").
    </p>

    <h2 id="investments">Investments</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/holdings/">Investments</a> comes from the financial disclosure reports House members file with the{" "}
      <a href="https://disclosures-clerk.house.gov/FinancialDisclosure">Clerk of the House</a>: an annual report each May
      (or August, with an extension) listing what they, their spouses and dependent children owned at the end of the
      year, and a transaction report within 45 days of any stock or bond trade over $1,000. Senators file with the Senate,
      whose search is not yet included.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reports give a <strong>range</strong> for every value, like $15,001–$50,000, never a figure, so totals here are
      ranges too: the low ends added up, and the high ends. Each holding and trade says <strong>whose</strong> it is.
      Mutual funds, ETFs and retirement accounts are listed by name only, so they aren't counted toward any company or
      industry, and their trades don't have to be reported. Companies and industries come from matching names and tickers
      to SEC filings.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most members file online. Some file on <strong>paper</strong>, which the House posts as scanned images. Those are
      read by machine: the text is recognized and each checkbox is judged by how much ink is in it. A second reader,
      Anthropic's Claude model, then reads each page on its own. A row is shown when the two readings agree, or when the
      second reader makes out a mark the first couldn't and agrees on everything else; a row the readers disagree on is
      left out until a person checks it against the page, and rows checked that way say so. Rows read this way are tagged <strong>Scan</strong> and link to their page of
      the original, and may still contain mistakes, so check the original before relying on one. Handwritten pages
      aren't read. Of the brokerage statements some
      members attach instead of listing their assets, Edward Jones and JPMorgan year-end statements are read, and only
      when the holdings add up to the statement's own totals to the cent; their exact values are shown as ranges like
      everything else. Tax forms attached to reports aren't read: they show income and sales, not what's owned.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owning a stock isn't a conflict of interest by itself, and a company's PAC money and members' holdings of its stock
      are shown separately. Under{" "}
      <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/13107">5 U.S.C. § 13107</a>, these reports may not be obtained or
      used for any unlawful or commercial purpose (other than by news media), to determine a credit rating, or to solicit
      money for any political, charitable or other purpose. This data is not part of this site's{" "}
      <a href="/open-data/">open data</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="travel">Sponsored travel</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/travel/">Sponsored travel</a> lists trips that a private group paid for, taken by a member of Congress or
      a member's staff since the current Congress began. Members and staff may accept these trips when they're connected
      to official duties and the House or Senate Ethics Committee approves them in advance. Afterward the traveler files
      a report with the{" "}<a href="https://disclosures-clerk.house.gov/GiftTravelFilings">Clerk of the House</a> or the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.senate.gov/legislative/Public_Disclosure/gift-rule-travel.htm">Secretary of the Senate</a>,
      with the sponsor's own form saying what it paid for transportation, lodging, meals and anything else.
    </p>
    <p>
      The House publishes an index of every filing with the traveler, office, sponsor, destination and dates; the cost
      is read from the sponsor's form in each filing, usually from the form's own fields. The Senate's index lists the
      traveler, office and dates, and its filings are scans, so the sponsor and cost are read by machine. A cost read
      from a scan is shown only when two readings agree and every figure was read with its dollar sign, and is tagged{" "}
      <strong>Scan</strong>; other costs are left out, with a link to the filing. Sponsors are matched to organizations
      on this site only when the names are the same, so a trade group's education foundation stays separate from the
      trade group.
    </p>
    <p>
      These filings are made under House Rule XXV and Senate Rule XXXV and published under the Honest Leadership and
      Open Government Act of 2007. They aren't the financial disclosure reports covered by 5 U.S.C. § 13107.
    </p>

    <h2 id="state-groups">State political groups</h2>
    <p>
      Some political organizations report to the IRS instead of the FEC: groups organized under Section 527 of the tax
      code that work on state races, like the Democratic and Republican governors' associations, the attorneys general
      and state legislative committees, and single-state groups. Organization pages show what each company, union or
      group with a page here gave them, from the contributions the groups list on Form 8872, from the{" "}
      <a href="https://www.irs.gov/charities-non-profits/political-organizations/political-organization-filing-and-disclosure">IRS's
      bulk download</a>. Contributors are matched to organizations by name, only when the contributor is written as an
      organization; the page says how it was filed. An amended report replaces the one it amends, and reports filed on
      paper aren't in the download (groups that raise more than $50,000 a year must file electronically).
    </p>
    <p>
      Most of this money goes to state races, not to members of Congress, so it's kept apart from the totals here. Fees
      that campaigns pay ActBlue's processing company, which files as a political organization, aren't counted as gifts.
      Four members' legal defense funds file the same reports, and their pages show what they raised.
    </p>

    <h2 id="labeler">The Bluesky labeler</h2>
    <p>
      Members of Congress with a Bluesky account are labeled with their top funding sectors and organizations
      by the <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/contributions.fyi">@contributions.fyi</a> labeler.
      Subscribe to it to see the labels on their posts.{" "}
      <a href="/bluesky/">How the labels work, and every feed, list and starter pack we publish</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      This site counts visits with <a href="https://plausible.io/privacy-focused-web-analytics">Plausible</a>, which
      doesn't use cookies or store personal data. We see aggregate numbers only: how many people read a page, which
      sites sent them, and a few counts like how often search is used (never what was searched for). Nothing is
      shared with advertisers.
    </p>

    <p class="muted small">Data last updated {generatedAt.toLocaleDateString("en-US", { dateStyle: "long" })}.</p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { max-width: 680px; font-size: var(--fs-md); line-height: 1.65; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: var(--sp-7); padding-top: var(--sp-4); border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
  .prose p, .prose ol { margin: 0 0 var(--sp-4); }
  .prose li { margin-bottom: var(--sp-2); }
</style>
